{
  "id": "tc-024",
  "pattern": "type-coercion",
  "difficulty_rating": 1650,
  "explanation": "normalizeScores maps missing scores to 0 with `scores[i] || 0` — harmless on its own — but the filtering loop in averageValid keeps a value only `if (normalized[i])`, which treats 0 as falsy and drops it exactly like a missing value. A legitimate score of 0 is indistinguishable from 'no data' by this check. So the two real zeros get silently excluded: valid ends up [5, 10, 5] (length 3, sum 20), giving 6.666..., instead of averaging all five scores (sum 20 / 5 = 4) as the function's name promises.",
  "prompt": "averageValid is meant to average only the scores that are actually present. Step through and predict what it returns for [5, 0, 10, 0, 5].",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "function normalizeScores(scores) {\n  const normalized = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {\n    normalized.push(scores[i] || 0);\n  }\n  return normalized;\n}\nfunction averageValid(scores) {\n  const normalized = normalizeScores(scores);\n  const valid = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < normalized.length; i++) {\n    if (normalized[i]) {\n      valid.push(normalized[i]);\n    }\n  }\n  let total = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < valid.length; i++) {\n    total += valid[i];\n  }\n  return total / valid.length;\n}\nconsole.log(averageValid([5, 0, 10, 0, 5]));",
  "interaction": "scrubber",
